<p>Whether you have worn a denture for years or are facing the loss of several teeth for the first time, you deserve a replacement that stays put, looks like you, and lets you eat normally. At 3rd Set Smiles in Tempe, Dr. Matthew Phillips plans, fits and adjusts dentures in the same office where he places and restores dental implants, so every option, removable or fixed, is on the table at one consultation.</p>

<h2>Who dentures are for</h2>
<p>Dentures may be a good fit if you:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Are missing most or all of the teeth in your upper or lower jaw</li>
  <li>Have several failing teeth that can't be predictably saved</li>
  <li>Wear an older denture that has become loose, cracked or sore</li>
  <li>Want an affordable starting point, with the option to add implants later</li>
</ul>

<h2>Your denture options</h2>
<h3>Full dentures</h3>
<p>When all of the natural teeth in an arch are missing, a full denture replaces them. Modern materials and a digital workflow let us build dentures that look natural, not like the bulky, obvious dentures of decades past.</p>

<h3>Partial dentures</h3>
<p>If you still have several healthy teeth, a partial denture fills in the gaps without removing or grinding down what you have. Partials use small clasps or precision attachments to stay in place.</p>

<h3>Immediate (same-day) dentures</h3>
<p>If teeth need to come out first, you don't have to go without teeth while you heal. An immediate denture is made ahead of time and placed the same day as your extractions. Your gums and bone change shape as they heal, so expect a few adjustment visits and a reline, or a new final denture, once healing settles.</p>
<!-- Confirmed by Mark 2026-10-16: the office offers immediate (same-day) dentures after extractions. -->

<h3>Implant-retained overdentures</h3>
<p>A small number of implants can anchor a removable denture so it snaps firmly into place instead of relying on suction or adhesive. For many denture wearers this is a big step up in stability and chewing. Learn more about <a href="/services/implant-supported-dentures/">implant-supported dentures</a>.</p>

<h3>Fixed full-arch teeth (All-on-4 / All-on-X)</h3>
<p>For patients who want teeth that never come out, a full arch can be attached to implants and only removed by the dentist. See <a href="/services/all-on-4/">All-on-4 and full-arch dental implants</a>, or read <a href="/blog/all-on-4-vs-implant-supported-dentures/">All-on-4 vs implant-supported dentures</a> to compare the two.</p>

<h2>What your visits look like</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li><strong>Exam and treatment planning.</strong> We look at your gums, remaining teeth, bite and bone, and talk through what you want day to day.</li>
  <li><strong>Precise impressions and bite records</strong> so the denture is shaped around your mouth.</li>
  <li><strong>Try-in visits</strong> to check shade, tooth position and your smile before anything is finished.</li>
  <li><strong>Delivery and follow-up adjustments</strong> to fine-tune fit as your mouth settles in.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Living with your denture</h2>
<p>Clean your dentures daily, take them out overnight, and come in for a yearly exam so we can check the fit and your gums. Gums and bone change shape over time, so most dentures eventually need a reline or replacement. A loose denture causes sore spots and can speed up bone loss.</p>

